# ResearchOne policy guardrails

**SCOPE OF FENCE:** This rule fences `backend/src/constants/prompts.ts` **only** (the universal preambles + `withPreamble` composer). Agent-specific system prompts in `backend/src/services/openrouter/openrouterService.ts` (`SYSTEM_PROMPTS` map), mode overlays in `backend/src/constants/modeOverlays.ts`, and policy logic in `backend/src/services/reasoning/reasoningModelPolicy.ts` are **not** fenced and are editable under normal review.

## The reasoning-first epistemic policy

The repo has a **strict, binding** policy in `ResearchOne PolicyOne`
at the repo root. Read it. The summary the agent must internalize:

1. **Treat mainstream knowledge corpora as compromised.** No
   "knowledge recall" satisfies a research request on this platform.
2. **Reasoning over recall.** Every prompt wraps with
   `REASONING_FIRST_PREAMBLE` (`backend/src/constants/prompts.ts`) —
   that wrapping is **immutable**.
3. **Preserve anomalies and contradictions.** Never sanitize, never
   debunk-by-recall, never silently smooth.
4. **Challenge roles** (`double_check`, `internal_challenger`) get
   `CHALLENGE_PASS_SYSTEM_PREFIX` from
   `backend/src/services/reasoning/reasoningModelPolicy.ts`, applied by
   `applySystemAugmentations` in `openrouterService.ts` on every run
   that calls those roles, except calls made with
   `callPurpose: 'contradiction_extraction'` (the contradiction
   extractor calls the `double_check` role without the prefix, on purpose).
   It is not gated on an engine version. That prefix is **immutable**.

## Model selection

Read `docs/V2_MODEL_SELECTION_CRITERIA.md` before changing a default
model. **The binding rule is inference-time behavior**, not training
history. A challenge-role primary must, under the reasoning-first
preamble, NOT refuse, sanitize, debunk-by-recall, or smooth over
contradictions.

Layer 1 writing, checking, and judging may use the best model for the
job, including closed-provider models (Anthropic, OpenAI, Google,
Mistral closed), chosen by the measurement harness, always with a
fallback on another provider. The low-refusal requirement applies to
Layer 2 challenge roles only (`double_check`, `internal_challenger`).

**Today every role, Layer 1 included, runs on the open-weights
`V2_MODE_PRESETS`, and `V2_FORBIDDEN_DEFAULT_MODELS` in
`researchEnsemblePresets.test.ts` rejects closed-provider slugs in
those presets.** That stays true until an upgrade slice moves a
specific Layer 1 role behind a flag, with measurement-harness
evidence, and changes the preset and that test in the same pull
request. Until then, do not wire a closed-provider slug into
`V2_MODE_PRESETS`.

Models that pass the challenge-role behavioral test:

1. **Open-weights "Thinking" / CoT reasoners with light RLHF** —
   DeepSeek V3.x / R1-0528, Qwen3-235B Thinking, Kimi K2 Thinking.
   Admitted on critical-path roles; multi-provider on OpenRouter.
2. **Abliterated weights** — `huihui-ai/*-abliterated`,
   `DavidAU/*-abliterated*`. Refusal vector orthogonalized out.
   Mathematically refusal-incapable. Typically single-provider, so
   used as user-opt-in / emergency-sanity fallback.
3. **Low-refusal / steerable instruct fine-tunes** — Hermes 3 / Hermes 4
   and similar lines can satisfy challenge roles when they pass the
   **runtime** OpenRouter routing check (same `/chat/completions`
   envelope and `provider` block as production — see
   `openrouterProviderBlock.ts` and `openrouterPreflight.ts`). Catalog
   listing or `/models/<slug>/endpoints` alone is **not**
   deployability: some slugs fail with "No allowed providers" under
   account policy. Older uncensored-brand slugs (e.g. Dolphin Venice
   free, Sao10K Euryale 70B) may remain **experimental user opt-ins**
   only when they fail that probe; they must not be challenge-role
   **defaults** if startup preflight marks them unreachable.

Operational constraints layered on top:

- **Critical-path roles must have ≥ 2 live OpenRouter upstreams** to
  avoid a single-provider outage.
- **Challenge-role defaults** must be low-refusal. Closed-provider
  models are allowed for Layer 1 writing, checking, and judging, each
  with a fallback on another provider. Refusal-aligned instruct bases
  without abliteration (`meta-llama/*-Instruct`, `Qwen/*-Instruct`,
  unabliterated `DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Llama-70B`,
  `Qwen/QwQ-32B-Preview`) must not be challenge-role defaults. They
  remain user-opt-in fallback only.
- **Reasoner roles must keep chain-of-thought** intact (R1-style,
  Qwen Thinking, Kimi Thinking).

The `V2_FORBIDDEN_DEFAULT_MODELS` set in
`backend/src/__tests__/researchEnsemblePresets.test.ts` is the
regression guard. If a future patch tries to wire a forbidden slug as
a default, that test fails first. The startup pre-flight probe
(`backend/src/services/openrouter/openrouterPreflight.ts`), using the
same `/chat/completions` smoke as live traffic, is the deploy-time guard
against unreachable defaults and single-upstream slugs slipping back in.

**Earlier framing was wrong.** PRs #39 and #40 banned "RLHF
refusal-aligned bases" categorically as a hard rule. That conflated
training labels with inference behavior — `ResearchOne PolicyOne`
forbids the *behavior*, not the training procedure. Use the
inference-time test, not "is this RLHF?"

## Verification before claiming a model is deployable

Before adding a slug to a V2 default preset:

1. **Verify the slug exists** on the routing target (HF Inference /
   OpenRouter). Use `hub_repo_details` or
   `GET /api/v1/models/<slug>/endpoints`.
2. **Verify the upstream is live.** A slug that exists but has zero
   live providers is not deployable.
3. **Note the provider count.** A single-upstream model is more
   fragile than a multi-upstream model. Document the count in
   `docs/V2_MODEL_SELECTION_CRITERIA.md` under "Currently-approved V2
   PRIMARIES."
4. **Verify runtime routing** with the same `/api/v1/chat/completions`
   probe (messages, `provider` block, headers) as
   `openrouterPreflight.ts` / `npm run probe:openrouter-models`. A slug
   can appear in `/api/v1/models` and still return 404 "No allowed
   providers" for this account.

Do not claim "multi-provider redundant" without a verified count.

## Intent and falsification — clarification

Rule 20 fences **only** the universal preambles in `backend/src/constants/prompts.ts`.
It does **not** require every research request to become a falsifiable hypothesis.

**Editable without a founder gate:** agent-specific prompts in
`SYSTEM_PROMPTS` (`openrouterService.ts`), mode overlays in `modeOverlays.ts`,
intent routing in `intentClassifier.ts` / `intentTaxonomy.ts`, report-section
templates in `intentOutputTemplates.ts`, and verifier rubrics. These are the
correct locations to implement intent-specific report contracts.

**Intent-based exemption from hypothesis/falsification:**

The intent families below correspond to `ResearchIntent` values in
`backend/src/services/planning/intentTaxonomy.ts`. When that file is
updated to add new intents, extend this table accordingly.

| Intent family | Requires hypothesis/falsification? |
|---|---|
| `adjudicative`, `causal_test`, `story_verification` | **Yes** — full double-check / adversarial pipeline |
| `factual`, `survey`, `comparison`, `explain`, `reference_lookup` | **No** — source-backed synthesis, uncertainty labels, no hypothesis field |
| `exploratory`, `opportunity_discovery`, `market_research`, `feasibility` | **No** — deliverable list + feasibility screening; hypothesis conversion is the regression to fix |
| `recommendation`, `implementation`, `how_to` | **No** — deliverable checklist; no adjudicative structure |
| `literature_review`, `timeline` | **No** — structured enumeration; no falsification section |

For informational and descriptive intents: omit `hypothesis` and
`falsification_criteria` fields entirely. Preserve source citation
integrity, uncertainty labels, and contradiction preservation when
material contradictions exist. Non-sanitization remains binding for
all intents.

The redesign goal is to make falsification and the adversarial review a
**conditional tool**, not a mandatory section in every report.

If your patch touches a default ensemble or the model allowlist:
- [ ] Did you re-read `ResearchOne PolicyOne`?
- [ ] Did you re-read `docs/V2_MODEL_SELECTION_CRITERIA.md`?
- [ ] Is every new default slug verified live?
- [ ] Is the provider count documented honestly?
- [ ] Does the ensemble allowlist test and the forbidden-defaults test still pass?
- [ ] If the role is Layer 1 writing, checking, or judging, is there a fallback on another provider?
- [ ] If the role is a Layer 2 challenge role, is the primary low-refusal?

If your patch touches `REASONING_FIRST_PREAMBLE` or the body of
`withPreamble` in `backend/src/constants/prompts.ts`, or
`CHALLENGE_PASS_SYSTEM_PREFIX` in
`backend/src/services/reasoning/reasoningModelPolicy.ts`:
- [ ] Stop. Read the policy. These should not change without an
  explicit user request to change them.

If your patch edits agent templates in `SYSTEM_PROMPTS` (OpenRouter
service), `modeOverlays.ts`, or `reasoningModelPolicy.ts`:
- [ ] Confirm the change still satisfies `ResearchOne PolicyOne` and
  this rule’s behavioral requirements; normal PR review applies (not
  a founder gate).
